{{History of Balisca}}

The '''Baliscan post-revolution exodus''' was the decades long continuous emigration of [[Baliscans]] from the [[Baliscan archipelago]] that followed the conclusion of the [[Baliscan Revolution]] in 1921. Throughout the exodus, millions of Baliscans, typically from elite economic and social positions, emigrated. This was either for political and ideological reasons or for fear of retaliation by the winning side and the revolutionary political regime [[Processo Revolucionario en Curso|taking shape]] in Balisca. Thus, they remained abroad until circumstances had changed in the country, which allowed them to return gradually. However, many (typically [[W:Upper class|upper class]]) became integrated into the societies that had given them refuge and thus they contributed to their development in some cases.

The main destination countries were, in particular, [[Argentina]], [[Brazil]], [[Kartasugih]] and [[Mexico]], but large groups were also granted asylum in other countries such as [[W:Uruguay|Uruguay]], [[W:Chile|Chile]], [[W:Colombia|Colombia]], [[W:Venezuela|Venezuela]], [[W:Cuba|Cuba]], [[W:Peru|Peru]], the [[W:Dominican Republic|Dominican Republic]], the [[United States]], and the [[United Kingdom]].

The emigrants in the exodus known as the "[[Baliscan diaspora|Baliscan exiles]]" have come from various backgrounds in Baliscan society, often reflected in the wave of emigration they participated in. Exiles have constructed Baliscan communities that continue to preserve their culture abroad, as well as garnering political influence outside of Balisca. Over the years, the internal political evolution in Balisca and the gradual process of [[Reconciliation council (Balisca)|reconciliation]] slowly allowed for the return of many of the exiles, typically those of [[W:Working class|working class]] background that faced discrimination and skepticism from that societies that had earlier welcomed them.

